<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part IV &mdash; Torus and Möbius &middot; Chapter 6</p>
  <h1>The Shape of Everything</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">The circle is π applied once: a line curved into a closed loop. The torus is π
    applied twice: a loop curved around another loop. Everything that must both circulate and return
    arrives at the same surface.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Why this shape, and not another</p>
    <h2 id="t-why-torus">A Loop Around a Loop</h2>
    <p class="lede">A sphere lets a path return but gives it nowhere to circulate. A cylinder lets a path
      circulate but never brings it home. The torus is the simplest surface that permits both at once
      &mdash; which is why anything that has to keep going round <em>and</em> come back ends up on
      one.</p>
    <p>The framework does not treat the recurrence as a coincidence noticed after the fact. It treats the
      torus as the second application of the same closure constant that made existence possible, and then
      goes looking for it. What it finds is a genuinely remarkable list &mdash; and the honest question,
      pressed in the ledger below, is whether that list is evidence or simply what you get when a shape is
      common enough. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established</span> &middot;
      <span class="hist">&#9670; scholarship</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; the framework's argument, contested</span> &middot;
      <span class="myth">&#9670; vision, stated as vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

