<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; what a second loop buys</p>
    <h2 id="t-why-two-loops">Why One Loop Is Not Enough</h2>
    <p>A circle closes a line. It gives return without repetition: you come back to where you were, and
      there is no room in the figure for the fact that you have been round once already. A circle cannot
      carry history, because every point on it is identical to itself on every pass.</p>
    <p>Curve that loop around a second loop and the situation changes completely. On a torus a path is
      labelled by two winding numbers &mdash; how many times round the tube, how many times round the ring
      &mdash; and the pair of them is a memory. Returning to the same point of the surface after a different
      winding is not the same event, and the figure knows it. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \begin{aligned}
      x&=(R+r\cos v)\cos u,\\
      y&=(R+r\cos v)\sin u,\\
      z&=r\sin v,
      \end{aligned}
      \qquad (u,v)\in[0,2\pi)^2,\quad (p,q)\in\mathbb Z^2
    \]</p>
    <p class="lede">The torus is the smallest shape that can both come back and keep count. That is the
      whole reason the framework needs π applied twice rather than once.</p>
    <p>Everything the structure is later asked to do rests on that one property. The spiral can ascend
      without leaving, because the ring direction carries the climb while the tube direction carries the
      return. A density can be a turn rather than a place. And a path can be non-contractible &mdash;
      impossible to shrink to a point &mdash; which is the precondition for the geometric phase that the
      <a href="/moebius/#t-cp">M&ouml;bius chapter</a> asks to do the work of CP violation. A contractible
      loop accumulates nothing.</p>
    <p class="muted">What is not claimed: that the universe is <em>literally</em> a torus in the sense a
      topologist would test. Cosmological searches for the matched circles a compact topology would print
      on the microwave background have found nothing, and the framework does not pretend otherwise. The
      torus here is the shape of the <em>process</em> &mdash; return with accumulation &mdash; and the
      distinction is kept rather than blurred. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>
